{
  "title": "Software Becomes Free to Copy",
  "subtitle": "In one month, a solo developer cloned seven Adobe apps with AI, and a hobby project started turning PS5 games into ordinary PC programs. I think copying software is about to become nearly free.\n\nThis is my best guess at how the next four years go, and where the value moves when the code itself stops being worth much.",
  "span": "2026 → 2030",
  "summary": "AnyPS5 and the ArtCraft Adobe clones show copying getting cheap. Where does value move next, and where do I stand?",
  "author": "Samartha",
  "started": "Oct 12, 2026",
  "updated": "Oct 12, 2026",
  "questions": [
    {
      "q": "What is this?",
      "a": "A story about how the next four years might go once AI makes rebuilding software cheap. The first chapter is reported from the news. Every chapter after it is my forecast, labelled as such."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does it matter to me?",
      "a": "If code stops being a moat, the skills and bets that made sense for my parents' generation of programmers stop making sense for me. I want to see where the value moves before I commit years to anything."
    },
    {
      "q": "How sure am I?",
      "a": "Not very. The direction feels solid; the speed is a guess. The dashboard numbers after October 2026 are my estimates, not data."
    },
    {
      "q": "What would change my mind?",
      "a": "A court ruling that AI-assisted 'clean-room' rebuilds infringe copyright, or the clones stalling in alpha for a year. Either would slow everything in this story down."
    }
  ],
